architecture-audit/cycle-021-selection-delete-command-kernel-boundary/reviews/stage-review.md:3:Status: PASS — no blockers.
architecture-audit/cycle-021-selection-delete-command-kernel-boundary/reviews/stage-review.md:9:3. `SelectionUseCases` direct command construction is handled concretely in proposal form without implementation, while broader command/persistence/kernel redesign is explicitly bounded or reserved for restructure/future review: **PASS**.
architecture-audit/cycle-021-selection-delete-command-kernel-boundary/reviews/round-01-review.md:3:**Verdict:** BLOCKING ISSUES
architecture-audit/cycle-021-selection-delete-command-kernel-boundary/reviews/round-01-review.md:7:- Reviewed artifacts: `scope-check.md`, `proposals/proposal-direct.md`, `proposals/proposal-restructure.md`, `architecture-audit/cycle-021-wave-plan-extension/plan-extension-note.md`, `architecture-audit/wave-plan.md`, current source files `frontend/src/application/appShell/SelectionUseCases.ts`, `frontend/src/application/appShell/KeyboardShortcutRouter.ts`, `frontend/src/application/appShell/AppShellRuntime.ts`, `frontend/src/commands/factories/DeletionSequenceFactory.ts`, and `frontend/src/commands/CommandStack.ts`.
architecture-audit/cycle-021-selection-delete-command-kernel-boundary/reviews/round-01-review.md:8:- Current dirty adjacent files remain pre-existing and out of scope for this review/next implementation unless a reviewed delta broadens scope: `frontend/src/commands/Command.ts`, `frontend/src/commands/factories/DeletionSequenceFactory.ts`, `frontend/src/commands/factories/PointSequenceFactory.ts`, `frontend/src/commands/operations/IOperation.ts`, and several world operation files (`post-t1-scoped-status.stdout`).
architecture-audit/cycle-021-selection-delete-command-kernel-boundary/reviews/round-01-review.md:13:`SelectionUseCases` still directly imports and constructs command-kernel primitives (`SelectionUseCases.ts:2-4`) and performs the full delete lifecycle inline: read selection, `new Command`, `DeletionSequenceFactory.createCascadeDeletion(...)`, `commit()`, `commandStack.push(...)`, and `clearSelection()` (`SelectionUseCases.ts:33-40`). That is the exact residual debt Wave 020 left behind and the correct center of this wave.
architecture-audit/cycle-021-selection-delete-command-kernel-boundary/reviews/round-01-review.md:15:The broader command-kernel redesign stop condition is also supported by source evidence: multiple other application modules still build commands directly, but Wave 021 only promises to remove the selection/delete leak first. `AppShellRuntime` is still an acceptable composition point for concrete `World` and `CommandStack` wiring in this wave (`AppShellRuntime.ts:186-209`). Keyboard routing should stay split: delete/select-all route through selection, undo/redo remain on `CommandStack` (`KeyboardShortcutRouter.ts:119-122`, `KeyboardShortcutRouter.ts:162-168`).
architecture-audit/cycle-021-selection-delete-command-kernel-boundary/reviews/round-01-review.md:21:1. **Advances target outcome:** **Pass**. It removes the exact leak in `SelectionUseCases.deleteSelected()` without dragging unrelated command paths into scope.
architecture-audit/cycle-021-selection-delete-command-kernel-boundary/reviews/round-01-review.md:22:2. **Improves architecture/code quality:** **Pass**. A dedicated selection-delete command service would give the delete seam a named owner and reduce app-shell knowledge of `Command`/`DeletionSequenceFactory` internals.
architecture-audit/cycle-021-selection-delete-command-kernel-boundary/reviews/round-01-review.md:23:3. **Respects no-backcompat / aggressive deletion:** **Pass**. The proposal explicitly deletes direct imports/calls from `SelectionUseCases` and rejects dual-path compatibility wrappers.
architecture-audit/cycle-021-selection-delete-command-kernel-boundary/reviews/round-01-review.md:26:6. **Validation sufficiency:** **Mixed**. The command-boundary test list is directionally correct, but it needs an explicit requirement to prove the new service/executor preserves `CommandStack.push()` contract behavior rather than merely exercising happy-path deletion.
architecture-audit/cycle-021-selection-delete-command-kernel-boundary/reviews/round-01-review.md:31:2. **Improves architecture/code quality:** **Pass**. A tiny `ApplicationCommandExecutor` could centralize repeated commit/push choreography and leave `SelectionUseCases` cleaner than today.
architecture-audit/cycle-021-selection-delete-command-kernel-boundary/reviews/round-01-review.md:35:6. **Validation sufficiency:** **Mixed**. Like the direct proposal, it needs an explicit semantic-preservation test requirement for stack/history behavior, not only focused deletion tests.
architecture-audit/cycle-021-selection-delete-command-kernel-boundary/reviews/round-01-review.md:37:## Blocking issues
architecture-audit/cycle-021-selection-delete-command-kernel-boundary/reviews/round-01-review.md:39:1. **Validation and test scope do not yet explicitly require semantic-preservation checks for the command lifecycle boundary being extracted.** Both proposals say they will cover commit/push/undo/redo integration, but neither proposal commits to verifying the concrete `CommandStack.push()` invariants that matter here: only committed commands can be pushed, non-undoable command behavior is preserved, redo clearing/notification timing stays unchanged, and selection is not cleared on a failed command build/push path. Because the wave’s whole architectural move is to relocate this choreography, vague “integration” coverage is not enough.
architecture-audit/cycle-021-selection-delete-command-kernel-boundary/reviews/round-01-review.md:46:  - undo/redo stack counts and redo invalidation remain consistent with current `CommandStack.push()` behavior;
architecture-audit/cycle-021-selection-delete-command-kernel-boundary/reviews/round-02-review.md:3:**Verdict:** NO BLOCKING ISSUES
architecture-audit/cycle-021-selection-delete-command-kernel-boundary/reviews/round-02-review.md:11:## Round-01 blocker resolution check
architecture-audit/cycle-021-selection-delete-command-kernel-boundary/reviews/round-02-review.md:13:Round 01 required both proposals to make semantic-preservation validation explicit for the extracted command lifecycle boundary. That blocker is now resolved in both proposals.
architecture-audit/cycle-021-selection-delete-command-kernel-boundary/reviews/round-02-review.md:19:- **Undo/redo counts and redo invalidation preserve current `CommandStack.push()` behavior:** both proposals now explicitly call for stack-count, redo-invalidation, and notification-timing assertions rather than generic integration coverage.
architecture-audit/cycle-021-selection-delete-command-kernel-boundary/reviews/round-02-review.md:20:- **Committed-only and non-undoable push behavior stay explicit:** both proposals now name these `CommandStack` contract invariants directly.
architecture-audit/cycle-021-selection-delete-command-kernel-boundary/reviews/round-02-review.md:28:1. **Advances target outcome:** **Pass**. It still removes the exact leak in `SelectionUseCases.deleteSelected()` with the narrowest change surface.
architecture-audit/cycle-021-selection-delete-command-kernel-boundary/reviews/round-02-review.md:29:2. **Improves architecture/code quality:** **Pass**. It introduces a named owner for selection/delete command construction while keeping `SelectionUseCases` focused on orchestration.
